31,2,,status,Benjamin,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
32,2,,status,Shunichi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
33,2,,status,Jonathan,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
34,2,,status,Asuka,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
35,2,1,talk,Asuka,,It seems that the barrier to entry is higher than we thought; many potential members are deterred by the commitment of a full year's fee upfront.,"{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,The administrative side is even more strained than I feared; chasing individual payments is a massive time sink compared to a single annual collection.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"My findings confirm a significant imbalance; a small group of regulars handles most of the attendance and the workload, while many others barely appear.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Shunichi,,It's clear that charging per event has a negative effect; turnout for weekday meetings dropped and never recovered after we introduced door charges.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Takumi,,"The ledger shows that seventy percent of our costs are fixed, regardless of attendance, and the old named-grade system only caused friction and discouraged people.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Benjamin, if chasing individual payments is such a burden, we have to be careful about any system that increases the number of transactions per person.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Asuka, precisely. Any move toward activity-based charging would likely be a nightmare for the treasurer, given the hours already spent chasing small sums.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Benjamin, I understand the workload, but we can't ignore that a few people are essentially subsidizing the rest. It's creating a visible divide in the club.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@Jonathan, but if we make it too ""fair"" by charging per event, we risk killing the atmosphere entirely. People stop coming if they have to weigh the cost every single time.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Jonathan, the ""subsidy"" is a myth if seventy percent of our costs are fixed. Whether one person or twenty show up, the hall hire and insurance stay the same. We can't charge per visit for fixed costs.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Takumi, that's a critical point. If the majority of our costs are fixed, a purely activity-based system wouldn't even cover the basics unless the base fee is high, which brings us back to the barrier for newcomers.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Asuka, exactly. And if we set a high base fee to cover those fixed costs, we're right back to the problem of people being deterred by a large upfront payment.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Benjamin, then we are stuck between a rock and a hard place. If we keep a flat fee, the regulars feel cheated; if we charge per event, we kill the vibe and bury the treasurer in paperwork.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@Jonathan, maybe the answer isn't about ""cheating"" but about belonging. If we make it too transactional, we lose the community. We need a way to keep it simple and welcoming.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Takumi,,"@Shunichi, ""belonging"" doesn't pay the insurance. We need a system that covers the fixed costs without creating the friction the old named-grade system did. Simplicity is key, but it has to be financially viable.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Asuka,,"We need to find a middle ground that doesn't scare off newcomers or burn out the treasurer, while still acknowledging the fixed costs Takumi mentioned.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"@Asuka, I agree. We must avoid any structure that multiplies the treasurer's workload or creates a financial wall that prevents people from joining in the first place.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I still worry about the regulars, but if the alternative is a dead club or a bankrupt treasurer, we have to prioritize stability over perfect equity.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Shunichi,,"I agree. If we prioritize the feeling of belonging and keep the process seamless, the regulars might be more willing to accept the current imbalance for the sake of the club's health.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Takumi,,"Let's just make sure whatever we pick doesn't rely on guesswork. We need a structure that covers those fixed costs reliably without making people fight over their ""grade"" again.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
